      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hufsmith was founded in 1872 as a station stop midway on the International‑Great Northern railroad line from Spring to Navasota.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hufsmith-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1963 Hufsmith boasted a tavern, liquor store, laundry, cafe, and two gas stations; by the 1980s only a cemetery and abandoned station remained.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hufsmith-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office operated in Hufsmith from 1902 until the 1980s, serving the community for nearly eight decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hufsmith-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early residents included Black settlers after the Civil War, such as Anderson King, a former slave who donated land for a school.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hufsmith-tx",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1936 the community had two churches, a school, and a sawmill.",
